[center][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/fo4-ocbpc@4fd97d389a06af8af74607de29174308dd41a491/docs/img/lips.jpg[/img][/center]
[center][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/fo4-ocbpc@4fd97d389a06af8af74607de29174308dd41a491/docs/img/crown.jpg[/img][/center]
[center][b]See it move:[/b] [url=https://pixeldrain.com/u/drJqN7wh]the inner path[/url] · [url=https://pixeldrain.com/u/LkzCZdiX]the throat path and deep face[/url] · [url=https://pixeldrain.com/u/gDAvHrok]lips that fit[/url] · [url=https://pixeldrain.com/u/6XN7D8kp]expressions and eye contact[/url] [i](adult; short loops are in the gallery)[/i][/center]
[center][i]The head's crown is measured from the real mesh, and both the tube and the lips read it.[/i][/center]

[size=5][b][color=#ffb547]With Rapport[/color][/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Rapport's face is the one that shows[/b] in any AAF scene. It is written after the engine's own merge, so it can close eyelids and a jaw an animation opened, which an expression override never can. Blinks still close the eyes.
[*][b]Spoken lines keep their lip sync[/b], and a held face eases in and out over a quarter of a second.
[*][b]Eyes that meet.[/b] Fallout 4 eyes have no bones: the game slides the eye's texture. The engine hooks that slide, so during a scene she looks up into her partner's eyes for 4-7 seconds now and then (Anatomy's config switches the glances on).
[/list]

[center][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/fo4-ocbpc@4fd97d389a06af8af74607de29174308dd41a491/docs/img/glances.jpg[/img][/center]

[size=5][b][color=#ffb547]Before you play[/color][/b][/size]

[quote]
[b]Fallout 4 1.10.163 with F4SE 0.6.23[/b] (Steam or GOG), or [b]the Anniversary Edition 1.11.x[/b] with [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/fallout4/mods/42147]F4SE[/url] 0.7.9. Both need [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/fallout4/mods/108394]Runtime Database[/url]: one cbp.dll for both. Every hook reads the game's code before patching it and stays off, logged, on any other build.

[b]Body, skeleton and physics preset.[/b] Anatomy is built for CBBE's own physics body and, since 1.2.0, for 3BBB (with Skeletal Adjustments for CBBE); other women's bodies aren't supported. Any skeleton works, 3BBB included: the genital bones are added at run time. Your ocbp.ini and collision file keep working unchanged; only [font=Courier New]cbp.dll[/font] must be this engine's.

[b]With Anatomy, a physics preset is optional:[/b] with none, Anatomy uses its own default preset; with your own [font=Courier New]ocbp.ini[/font], yours is used as before.

[b]Install with Vortex or MO2; manual installs are not supported.[/b]

[b]It replaces cbp.dll.[/b] If OCBPC, Jiggle Physics or another mod also ships [font=Courier New]F4SE\Plugins\cbp.dll[/font], let this one win in your mod manager. Keep their configs: they are read as they are.

[b]The log.[/b] [font=Courier New]Documents\My Games\Fallout4\F4SE\anatomy_ocbpc.log[/font] records what was set up, once per run. A discovery log for modders, which lists unknown genital- or toy-looking bones nearby, is off unless you turn it on ([font=Courier New][Log] discover=1[/font]).
[/quote]

[size=5][b][color=#ffb547]Incompatibilities[/color][/b][/size]

Known conflicts, from real reports and the code. Found another one? Tell us in the Bugs tab.
[list]
[*][b]Other mods shipping F4SE\Plugins\cbp.dll (OCBPC, Jiggle Physics 82699, and many physics presets)[/b]: whichever dll wins decides, and an older one turns every Anatomy feature off. Let the Anatomy Engine's cbp.dll win and keep their ocbp.ini / collision files; those are read unchanged.[/*]
[*][b][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/fallout4/mods/105699]OCBP LPCE[/url][/b]: a separate physics engine on the same bones. Run one, not both.[/*]
[*][b]Men's skeletons other than [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/fallout4/mods/36702]ZeX - ZaZ Extended Skeleton[/url][/b]: the penis aim needs ZeX's linked penis bones (Penis_00 to Penis_05 in one chain). With any other men's skeleton the aim switches itself off (the log says so) and the penis keeps the animation's own pose. Use ZeX 6.0 for the men.[/*]
[/list]

[size=5][b][color=#ffb547]FAQ[/color][/b][/size]

[b]Do I need Anatomy?[/b]
[spoiler]No. On its own it is OCBPC with the once-per-frame collision fix and safer hooks. Everything genital, the mouth and the aim is switched on by Anatomy's config.[/spoiler]

[b]Why is the source public?[/b]
[spoiler]F4SE requires a plugin's source to be public, and it is also how you can check this DLL: [url=https://github.com/ReidenXerx/fo4-ocbpc]github.com/ReidenXerx/fo4-ocbpc[/url]. The fork's changes are GPL-3.0 on top of OCBPC's MIT base, and every changed file says what changed and when.[/spoiler]

[b]Where is every setting?[/b]
[spoiler]In the sticky posts: each ini section, what it turns on, and what stays off unless you set it.[/spoiler]

[b]Isn't FSMP better?[/b]
[spoiler]For some things, yes, and they are different tools. FSMP (SMP) is a full physics simulation: cloth, hair, and collision between actual meshes, set up per mesh in XML. For hair and clothing it is the stronger choice, and it works alongside this engine: SMP for hair and cloth, this engine for the body.
What Anatomy needs is not a physics simulation alone. This engine adds the genital bones while the game runs, opens the body by contact with a stretch model, collides a penis as one smooth tube, aims it into the opening the scene means along measured canal paths, fits the lips to what is in the mouth, and (with Rapport) drives her face and eye contact. A physics simulation doesn't do aiming, faces or eyes. The engine also runs on 1.10.163 and the Anniversary Edition from one DLL, and spring bones are typically lighter on the CPU than a full simulation, which helps on weaker PCs and the Steam Deck.[/spoiler]

